      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 5114298124278996232, guid: c800375dfcf2b054db4f0d8af5576733, type: 3}
      propertyPath: storyText
      value: "\xBBThe idea of the nativeness of plants was developed in the mid nineteenth
        century by English botanists with direct reference to concepts of civic rights
        in English Common Law.\xAB\r\n \r\n\xBB[Who are] those that do not belong,
        that migrate without permission, those that are seen as a threat, those that
        do not stick to the rules, those that are \u203Atoo much\u2039 and \u203Atoo
        many,\u2039 those that reproduce without permission.\xAB\r\n \r\n\xBBAnd
        \u203AWhat role does naming play, especially when it is a matter of crossing
        boundaries: boundaries of species, of states, of classification, of belonging.\u2039\xAB\r\n\r\n\u2013
        Nanna Heidenreich (What\u2019s in a Name?, 2022) \u2013"
      objectReference: {fileID: 0}
    - target: {fileID: 5114298124278996232, guid: c800375dfcf2b054db4f0d8af5576733, type: 3}
